  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1049 PM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nThunderstorms today mostly stayed focused east of the Rio Grande,\nunder an upper tropospheric deformation zone associated with an\ntight upper tropospheric low over northern Coahuila. This fit\nquite well with the Pytlak (2005) conceptual model of upper \ntropospheric lows and troughs in relation to modulating monsoon\nseason precipitation. Higher PoPs in put in the forecast 24 hours\nago, largely leaning into pattern recognition and supported by\nCAMS, were the right call versus NBM output which produced PoPs at\nELP below climatological PoP values for the date.\n\nWith the upper low's westward progress stalled, as mentioned last\nnight, Sunday continues to look like an active day with\nthunderstorm coverage a little more evenly spread out across the\narea. The upper level deformation zone will be laid SW to NE\nacross the CWA from the Bootheel into the Texas Panhandle. An\nadded wrinkle will be increased upper divergence in SW New Mexico\nfrom a weak disturbance lifting across Arizona, as SW flow edges\ninto the western arm of the subtropical ridge. This will act in\ntandem with the upper tropospheric low. Models show the closed\nmid-level low will decouple from the upper circulation, drifting\ninto the Big Bend region Sunday afternoon, and leaving upper\ncirculation behind in Coahuila. A notable pocked of cooler mid-\nlevel air (-8C at 500 mb) will accompany the low as it drifts \ntowards us, but it looks to mostly stay out of area. The decoupled\nlow also has a weak reflection down to around 700 mb, and the GFS\nhas a plume of higher dewpoints across southern NM by afternoon.\n\nSo, in short, another active day tomorrow with showers and\nthunderstorms over a wider area, likely most of the CWA. CAMS\nsuggest max rainfall amounts in the 1 to 2 inch range. There's\nlittle consensus on who will get hit the hardest in the lowlands,\nbut some consensus favoring heavier rain chances in the Gila as \nwell as the Sacramento Mountains (especially the west slopes).\nWill hold off on any Watches, and let subsequent shifts chew on\nupdated guidance. \n\nBy Monday, the upper tropospheric trough will mostly be sheared\nout, but a remaining weakness leftover from the decoupled mid-\nlevel low will remain over southern New Mexico. This is also possibly\nsigns of an MCV over the area, and this is also hinted at in GFS\nCAPE values, with a relative minimum in instability over south-\ncentral New Mexico. If things are active tomorrow, someone is\nlikely to get \"shut off\" for Monday, and south-central NM (eastern\nLuna into Dona Ana County) is a fair bet. Best chances for\nthunderstorms will be in the Gila and far SW New Mexico into the \nBootheel, with a secondary focus over the Sacramento Mountains.\nPWAT values in the lowlands will still be hovering around 1.2 to\n1.3 inches, so the localized heavy rain threat will continue. \n\nWarmer and drier conditions look to take over from Tuesday, with\nstill hope for a relief as the ridge breaks down on its eastern\nedge later in the week.",
